WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:01.053
- [Narrator] In this video,

00:00:01.053 --> 00:00:05.570
we're going to cover what
criteria a non citizen must meet

00:00:05.570 --> 00:00:07.070
to become a citizen.

00:00:07.070 --> 00:00:09.920
A process we call naturalization.

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:13.460
Some of the requirements
are obvious and verifiable

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:18.040
while others are tested through
the naturalization process.

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:21.750
There are nine requirements
that an immigrant must meet

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:24.160
before they can apply for naturalization.

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:27.540
And four of the first five all have to do

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:31.620
with how long an applicant has
lived in the United States.

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:34.520
First, they have to be 18 years old.

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:37.770
Does this mean that children
can't become citizens?

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:40.680
No, but they can't apply on their own.

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:43.950
If their parent or parents
apply for naturalization,

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:46.670
a child can inherit their
parent's citizenship

00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:49.200
if they make it through the process.

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:53.380
Second, the applicant has to
have been a permanent resident

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:55.540
for at least five years,

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:57.530
unless they're married
to an American citizen

00:00:57.530 --> 00:00:59.380
then it's only three years.

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:01.860
So this means that undocumented immigrants

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:03.400
and foreign diplomats

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:06.610
are not eligible for naturalization.

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:08.660
Third, they have to
show that they've lived

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:10.010
in the US customs

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:13.020
and immigration services
district they've been living in

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:16.040
when they applied for
at least three months.

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:18.740
There are 26 US CIS districts

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:22.220
which also include US territories.

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:23.960
Fourth, they have to have lived

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:26.040
in the United States continuously

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:28.620
for at least five years.

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:32.260
A green card holder can leave
for short periods of time,

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:34.410
but if they've lived
outside the United States

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:36.130
for more than six months,

00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:40.120
they could compromise their
eligibility for naturalization.

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:42.900
Included in that is the fifth requirement

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:45.780
that they have to have
been physically present

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:46.940
in the United States

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:50.060
for at least 30 months
out of the five years

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:51.920
of residency they maintained

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:55.500
before they file their
application for naturalization.

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:58.060
These five cover the
residency requirements

00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:01.320
for naturalization, but there
are four other requirements

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:05.550
that are verified during the
naturalization process itself.

00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:08.670
If you remember the steps of
the naturalization process,

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:10.820
you should remember that at one point,

00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:13.980
a person seeking
naturalization needs to meet

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:17.220
with a US CIS official for an interview.

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:20.410
and to take the naturalization test.

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:21.580
It's during this step

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:24.840
that the US CIS official
tests the applicant

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:27.210
on the next two requirements,

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:29.450
their ability to understand English

00:02:29.450 --> 00:02:32.450
and their knowledge of US
history and government.

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:34.010
The federal government requires

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:38.380
that all naturalized citizens
can read, write, speak

00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:41.530
and understand conversational English.

00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:43.990
The interview itself serves as the test

00:02:43.990 --> 00:02:45.540
for the speaking portion.

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:49.010
And after the interview, the
applicant has to read one

00:02:49.010 --> 00:02:52.360
out of three chosen sentences correctly.

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:53.960
And for the writing test,

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:57.220
the applicant will write an
English sentence provided

00:02:57.220 --> 00:02:58.720
by the interviewer.

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:00.400
Following the English portion,

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:02.650
the interviewer asks 10 questions

00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:04.540
about US history and government

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:08.210
and the applicant must answer
at least six correctly.

00:03:08.210 --> 00:03:11.840
Some examples of questions that
an interviewer can ask are,

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:14.410
how many amendments does
the constitution have?

00:03:14.410 --> 00:03:17.760
Or, who's in charge of
the executive branch?

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:20.770
Then there are only two requirements left,

00:03:20.770 --> 00:03:22.680
and they're the hardest to verify

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:25.530
because they aren't entirely obvious.

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:28.080
The eighth requirement for
naturalization is that you have

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:31.260
to be a person of good moral character.

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:34.830
Trying to determine a person's
character is hard to do.

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:37.080
Do you think you could get a good grasp

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:39.450
of a person's character in an interview

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:41.040
that lasts a few hours

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:43.970
or reading an application they filled out?

00:03:43.970 --> 00:03:46.470
The way the government
determines moral character

00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:50.620
is by looking to see if an
applicant has a criminal history,

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:53.000
but that isn't exactly foolproof.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:56.140
They can look into American
criminal databases,

00:03:56.140 --> 00:03:57.390
but they don't have access

00:03:57.390 --> 00:03:59.580
to other countries' criminal records.

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:03.330
US CIS often has to
rely on self reporting.

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:07.050
And while it is rare for
someone with a criminal history

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:09.350
to make it through the
naturalization process,

00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:11.100
it has been known to happen.

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:12.320
The final requirement

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:15.760
is that the applicant has
to demonstrate an attachment

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:19.770
to the principles and ideals
of the US Constitution.

00:04:19.770 --> 00:04:22.100
This means that an applicant has to prove

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:24.790
that they would actively
support the constitution

00:04:24.790 --> 00:04:28.030
and support a democratic
form of government.

00:04:28.030 --> 00:04:30.810
If someone is hostile to
the form of government

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:34.180
in the United States, they
cannot become a citizen.

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:36.200
One way that a person proves this

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:39.320
is by agreeing to take
an oath of allegiance

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:40.930
in a public ceremony.

00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:41.763
In this oath,

00:04:41.763 --> 00:04:45.680
they must promise to renounce
all foreign allegiance,

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:47.260
though that does not mean they have

00:04:47.260 --> 00:04:48.560
to give up their citizenship

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:51.900
to other countries and promise
to give full allegiance

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:55.910
to the United States, its
constitution and its laws.

00:04:55.910 --> 00:05:00.160
They must also promise to perform
all duties and obligations

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:03.100
of citizenship including
defending the country

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:04.313
when called upon.

00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:07.840
So now that we've covered
all of the requirements,

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:09.370
let's go through an example

00:05:09.370 --> 00:05:11.450
to see if you can determine
whether an immigrant

00:05:11.450 --> 00:05:14.203
is eligible for citizenship or not.

00:05:15.570 --> 00:05:18.950
Lorenzo has lived most
of his life in Italy,

00:05:18.950 --> 00:05:21.750
but now he wants to become a US citizen.

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:24.110
He came to the United
States three years ago

00:05:24.110 --> 00:05:26.750
and has been a legal resident ever since.

00:05:26.750 --> 00:05:29.230
He has never been in trouble with the law,

00:05:29.230 --> 00:05:31.340
either in the United States or in Italy.

00:05:31.340 --> 00:05:33.330
He enjoys owning his own home

00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:35.350
and living in a friendly neighborhood.

00:05:35.350 --> 00:05:37.460
Lorenzo is 25 years old,

00:05:37.460 --> 00:05:40.920
speaks English very well and
has recently taken a class

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:43.650
at a local community college on US history

00:05:43.650 --> 00:05:44.950
for new citizens.

00:05:44.950 --> 00:05:49.570
He filled out his naturalization
application form last week.

00:05:49.570 --> 00:05:52.160
So let's check off all of the requirements

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:54.090
that Lorenzo does have.

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:55.870
Take a second and pause this video

00:05:55.870 --> 00:05:58.690
to try and figure it
out on your own first.

00:05:58.690 --> 00:06:01.490
All right, so Lorenzo is 25 years old

00:06:01.490 --> 00:06:04.580
so he meets the minimum age requirement.

00:06:04.580 --> 00:06:07.850
He's only been a permanent
resident for three years

00:06:07.850 --> 00:06:10.470
and he's not married to a US citizen

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:13.440
so he doesn't meet the
residency requirement.

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:15.670
He's lived in Portland for three years

00:06:15.670 --> 00:06:19.100
so he meets the third
and fifth requirements,

00:06:19.100 --> 00:06:22.340
but he doesn't meet
the fourth requirement.

00:06:22.340 --> 00:06:24.430
Lorenzo can speak English very well

00:06:24.430 --> 00:06:27.060
and took a class on US
history for new immigrants

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:30.690
so we can assume that he
can pass both portions

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:32.770
of the naturalization test.

00:06:32.770 --> 00:06:35.550
And since he's never been
in any legal trouble,

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:36.820
we'll put a check mark next

00:06:36.820 --> 00:06:39.340
to him having good moral character.

00:06:39.340 --> 00:06:42.630
We can't exactly tell if he's
attached to the principles

00:06:42.630 --> 00:06:45.000
and ideals of the US Constitution

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:48.590
so we'll put a question mark
next to that requirement.

00:06:48.590 --> 00:06:50.340
All right, given all this information,

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:52.510
do you think Lorenzo is eligible

00:06:52.510 --> 00:06:55.090
to apply for naturalization?

00:06:55.090 --> 00:06:57.120
If you answered no, you're correct.

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:00.750
Even though Lorenzo meets
a lot of the requirements,

00:07:00.750 --> 00:07:03.600
he doesn't meet all of
them and eligibility

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:05.440
is an all or nothing type deal.

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:09.250
He has to meet all nine
requirements before he can apply.

00:07:09.250 --> 00:07:11.930
In the next video, we'll
cover some of the challenges

00:07:11.930 --> 00:07:13.963
of the naturalization process.

